#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079433} :activity/id #uuid "c089972c-82c7-5567-870b-266e9a2781cf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050093 :topic/id #uuid "2ce94c98-9eb2-5a29-8564-48c618c36a1e" :topic/math-academy-id 577} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079433 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079678 :tutorial/content "In the coordinate plane, dilations are typically centered at the origin. When a dilation is centered at the origin, it can be represented by a function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (kx, ky)\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the scale factor of the dilation.\n\nFor example, let's compute the image of the point $(3,2)$ (shown below) under the action of a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $3.$ \n\n![](images/2d/2da731925da5705e15cb58abd632e52c04139e8052772eeaa3ca2862fd6d3d02.png)\n\nA dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $3$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(3x,3y\\right).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(3,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n(3,2) \\mapsto \\left(3\\cdot 3, \\:3 \\cdot 2 \\right) = (9,6).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(9,6).$\n\n![](images/94/9410030b5b309d512c937741e9fdb85abad0aacdd712f81ee0b50897523952cc.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "530dc6f3-a090-5cc7-ae83-525ede6c1e2c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4717 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "356ba1a3-2b60-500b-8fc1-84b042470ccf" :step/math-academy-id 29131 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079434}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079434 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078517 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078367 :question/id #uuid "c2883f5a-2126-51d9-b7bd-04bfd4418a3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7841"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "972bb75e-a787-58f1-83c0-1ae32aaf925d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050809}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535992}] :knowledge-point/title "Dilating Points in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "b61e657a-d112-5829-a63a-d3bd114efbd1" :step/math-academy-id 29132 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079435}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079435 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078518 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078368 :question/id #uuid "67b906da-2da2-570c-8a11-de5839c7686a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7842"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "261176ed-19bf-5d71-86b7-ba2e0ba31dc1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535997}] :knowledge-point/title "Dilating Figures in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "1b787bd9-ff80-5a23-9cd4-1ac7f89a1e06" :step/math-academy-id 29135 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079468}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079436 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078519 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078369 :question/id #uuid "7c567754-a661-5235-bde3-ae6cbff72ed5" :question/math-academy-id "e-7843"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f22cdd47-6fd4-5f15-b5a9-ce290e204a57" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535948}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Scale Factor of a Dilation in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "3d1661fe-98e8-5860-aad0-d3a355a77f07" :step/math-academy-id 29138 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079437}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079437 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079679 :tutorial/content "If $l$ is a line passing through the center of dilation, then the image $l'$ under the dilation is the same as the original line. \n\n![](images/eb/eb208ea1b492a1d471e0ffb6b3f23fa80a4747f3b5f6da1ad126c30716e52743.png)\n\nHowever, if $k$ is a line that does not pass through the center of dilation, then the image $k'$ is parallel to the original line.\n\n![](images/09/09a11211bde36f85dfe48e4a769314d084edc99c523b869ed3f9a518784fb9a6.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "23754991-48e0-5988-b048-bbdd32cf900d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4773 :tutorial/title "The Image of a Line Under a Dilation"} :step/id #uuid "834f7693-5561-5b04-a214-445eeb9553b3" :step/math-academy-id 29583 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079438}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079438 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078520 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078373 :question/id #uuid "b0ca5c59-6d06-5b5a-8b25-bd364fcb4bf5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8547"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2ce690-f8a9-5cc2-9baf-2d7163602589" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535935}] :knowledge-point/title "The Image of a Line Under a Dilation"} :step/id #uuid "c9322ea9-c9d9-50e1-94b8-d4dcd13fdd5a" :step/math-academy-id 31778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079468 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079703 :tutorial/content "Let's discuss how to find a center of a dilation\n\nThe center of a dilation lies on any line that passes through corresponding points on a figure and its image. Therefore, we can find a center of dilation as the intersection of any two of these lines. \n\nLet's look at some examples:\n\n- Consider the rectangle $ABCD$ and its image $A'B'C'D'$ under the action of a dilation, shown below.\n\n![](images/91/9115c6a43d20f58e8c2e4b9a77337edd18688dd831168405062c6e0fbe207bb0.png)\n\nWe can find the center of dilation $O$ by connecting two corresponding points with a straight line and finding their intersection. \n\nFor example, if we draw the lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AA'}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BB'}$ and find their intersection, we get the center of dilation $O$ shown below.\n\n![](images/4a/4a3b1ac04e7d549aafdcf50ea55e172f385fbb7a71bd061c74581ca50f94535a.png)\n\n- Sometimes, a figure and its image lie on opposite sides of the center of dilation. \n\nFor example, consider the rectangle $ABCD$ and its image $A'B'C'D'$ shown below. \n\n![](images/a8/a8918779d1b5152e7071bdb7ba7d349978b166ff613ec2c8b918f9c2ea5b0b92.png)\n\nIf we draw the lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AA'}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BB'},$ we find that the center of dilation $O$ lies between the two shapes.\n\n![](images/14/14c6bc0bd148d2ac61c42d4e1636c4301a7af77e773338358e51e4a297f40a48.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "332ed95e-8170-5e85-89b6-a7ea3befd30f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7688 :tutorial/title "Finding a Center of Dilation"} :step/id #uuid "65544bc7-c18a-5a7a-9bd2-24d87192b4c6" :step/math-academy-id 44629 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079436}}] :activity/title "Dilations of Figures in the Coordinate Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078625}]}
